// Q&A tables per segment number: { window, rows: [[question, answer, owner], ...] }
const QA = {
  1: { window: 'during the talk / 17:03–17:10', rows: [
    ['Why is Sprint 3 only “integrated” when the code was merged?', 'Our Definition of Done says "on dev" means shown on the deployed environment; a local run does not count. Checkout and scheduling were still off on dev at the freeze (#223), so the review recorded the goal as not met and carried it into Sprint 4. Evidence: sprint-03/review.md, sprint-03/plan.md §8.', 'Lim'],
    ['Two Sprint 2 Done rules are missing from your Sprint 3 list. Why?', 'They are “implementation matches the approved contract” and “health check passes and rollback remains available”. No decision to drop them is recorded. Both are still in the pipeline: the contract-drift check runs on backend and contract pull requests, though it is not a required check, and the dev deploy health-checks the new container and rolls back where a previous image exists (docs/agile/definition-of-done.md, changes between the sprint lists).', 'Lim'],
    ['Are these hours actuals?', 'The hours are plans. We planned in hours per story; done hours are planned hours of finished work (Sprint 2 report, Sprint 3 review), and the Sprint 4 burndown sums planned estimates of cards not yet Done. Nobody logged time; the actual column is in person-days, the split we agreed from the issues each person delivered.', 'Lim'],
    ['Was the effort balanced across the five members?', 'Within the SWE5001 course guideline of 8.5 to 12.5 person-days each, about 50 in total, the split the team agreed. The plans gave everyone 30 h in Sprints 2 and 3; Sprint 4 started at 38–51 h each. Board cards differ in size, for example one Stripe pull request was about 10,000 lines, so card counts are not effort.', 'Lim'],
    ['Why is the effort the same as in your SWE5001 talk?', 'BlocBuy is one project for both courses: the same repository, board and sprints. We agreed one effort split for the whole project, from the issues each person delivered, and presented it in SWE5001 on 21 Oct; counting it twice would overstate the work.', 'Lim'],
    ['The proposal promised JWT. Where is that change recorded?', 'In the backend skeleton plan of 30 Aug (decisions D6 and D7: a Sa-Token session in Redis, roles from SQL tables) and in frontend ADR 0002 (same-origin /api, HttpOnly cookie). We chose a server session because we can revoke it and the token never reaches JavaScript. There is no numbered backend ADR for it yet (open item 7).', 'Lim → Liu Jin'],
    ['Why did you build an admin GUI when the proposal excluded it?', 'The exclusion was a merchant self-service GUI. Admins needed to configure team terms on real listings to make START possible on dev (US25, #221), and an API-only flow was error-prone in the demo; the console reuses the same versioned API (frontend ADR 0015). It is extra scope, not a replacement.', 'Lim'],
    ['What is your biggest open risk for the demo?', 'The 16:05 refund landing late, or a dev page failing to load. Nothing is paid on air: D1–D3 show the purchase both buyers completed on dev on 24 Oct and D4 the 16:05 bloc; a page that fails plays the 24 Oct recording (#657). Every payment step has been shown on dev: paid START and the expiry refund on 23 Oct (PR #664), a second buyer’s paid JOIN on 24 Oct. Not shown on dev: crash recovery under replay (#224).', 'Lim → Yorik'],
  ] },
  2: { window: 'during the talk / 17:03–17:10', rows: [
    ['Why a monolith on dev and services on prod?', 'Decision: one codebase, two topologies (backend ADR 0008). Reason: SWE5006 grades a complete runnable system, so dev stays the monolith on one EC2 host; SWE5001 needs three services on ECS. The services are thin assemblies of the same modules, and ArchUnit holds the boundaries in the monolith too.', 'Eric'],
    ['Is ProductStatus really the State pattern, or an enum switch?', 'It is State. Each enum constant with a body compiles to its own subclass, overrides only the moves it allows and returns the next state; the default methods refuse with 409. Product calls its state and does not branch on it. Evidence: ProductStatus.java, ProductStatusTest. One switch remains: AdminListingAppServiceImpl.changeStatus picks publish or delist from the requested target. The team uses a transition table (DealStatus.canTransitionTo), which we do not call State.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['How do you stop a double refund?', 'A durable obligation, then an idempotent call. The obligation is unique per payment and commits with the expiry; the Stripe idempotency key is built from its ID; a lease plus a version fence stops two workers; after 23 hours the worker only searches Stripe, never creates. Evidence: RefundServiceTest.lostResponseRetriesSameDurableKey. Limitation: proven locally with a provider substitute, not on dev (#223). The states, from pending to succeeded, failed or review, are the refund obligation statechart in report §3.4.2 (statecharts.md).', 'Yorik'],
    ['Why a fixed 30 s retry and not exponential backoff?', 'A fixed delay in RefundMapper.xml: the idempotency key makes each retry safe, and the 23-hour cutoff ends automatic retries. There is no exponential backoff or jitter, so we call it a fixed delay.', 'Yorik'],
    ['Why a Factory Method with only one creator?', 'The creator is a seam: initiate is final, so any creator keeps the validation, and constructor injection (#421) lets a test or configuration supply another creator without editing the service. Limitation: one creator, chosen at build time; we do not claim runtime-pluggable creation.', 'Eric'],
    ['Strategy with only one strategy: is that not speculative?', 'It is a liability we state (SDP 05a p35). The seam exists because proposal §6.3 made new pricing schemes a primary design driver; the selection is data-driven (biz_product.pricing_mode) and the two contexts never name the algorithm. A second mode would add a constant, a class, a bean and tests, and leave browse and quote untouched. We do not claim it has been exercised.', 'Lim'],
    ['Why not the State pattern for the team, as the proposal said?', 'Only OPEN has behaviour. SUCCEEDED and EXPIRED are final and refuse every move with the same error. A transition table (DealStatus.canTransitionTo) and the guards in GroupDeal cover that; State would add three classes with no behaviour. Listings differ: each state changes how four operations behave (slide 18). Slide 4 records the change.', 'Liu Jin → Eric'],
    ['Is your repository or DAO a design pattern?', 'Not a GoF one, so it is not on the pattern slides. It is the persistence transition strategy (SAD 04a p64–65, the simple DAO; p75): domain repository interfaces, implemented in infrastructure by MyBatis mappers. GroupDeal has two, on purpose: GroupDealRepository for reads, and PaidGroupDealRepository, a write protocol that runs start, join, settlement and expiry in one transaction, locking the payment receipt and then the deal, so the webhook and the expiry sweeper cannot deadlock (ADR 0001, 0002). That bends one aggregate per transaction, a trade we accept for payment idempotency; its name says repository, but it is really a unit-of-work contract (report §3.2.4, §3.2.8). The same holds for the outbox, idempotency keys and the Optimistic Offline Lock: mechanisms, not GoF patterns.', 'Eric'],
    ['How do analysis and design differ in your UC4 models?', 'Analysis (slide 13) uses only boundary, control and entity, with attributes on entities and no platform (SAD 03a). Design (slide 14) has the real classes with typed signatures, each tagged with the transition strategy that produced it, and the sequence shows the transaction, the row locks and the guarded update. The full set is in report §3.4.1; report §3.2 maps each analysis object to its design elements (JoinTeamControl splits into five services by step; Refund becomes a biz_refund row with no domain class).', 'Eric'],
    ['Is there a database per service?', 'On dev, no: one PostgreSQL database holds every table in schema domain. On ECS, yes: one RDS instance with one database per service. Each database applies the full Flyway history, and each service role is granted only the tables it owns (ADR 0008). Full list: B4.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['Without foreign keys, what stops orphan rows?', 'The application. Our DDL rule bans foreign keys and CHECK constraints; that is a team rule, not a course rule. The reason: in the cloud each service has its own database (ADR 0008), so a link to another service’s table cannot be a foreign key anyway, and we keep one schema for both environments. Business tables carry a deleted flag, so a delete hides a row instead of breaking its links. Before a listing is deleted, the use case checks every reference: only a draft with no deals, quotes or price policies can go (AdminListingAppServiceImpl.delete, PRODUCT_REFERENCED). Unique indexes stop duplicate rows under races.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['Do notifications work on dev?', 'No, by decision: the dev host runs no broker and leaves the outbox writer, relay and consumer off (application-ec2.yml; ADR 0007 scope decision, 11 Oct). The path runs on ECS through SNS and SQS, and locally and in CI against ElasticMQ (OutboxRelayElasticMqIT, NotificationConsumerElasticMqIT).', 'Pan'],
  ] },
  3: { window: 'during the talk / 17:03–17:10', rows: [
    ['How do you test payments without calling Stripe?', 'Stripe sits behind two ports, the Checkout gateway and the Refund gateway. Unit tests replace the port. StripeRolloutAcceptanceIT (8 tests) runs the real HTTP endpoints, the scheduler, the Flyway schema and the webhook signature checks; only OAuth and the calls to Stripe are replaced. On dev on 23 Oct, a paid Sandbox START settled and a natural expiry refunded once (PR #664, merged 24 Oct, bef39b72); a second buyer’s paid JOIN was shown on dev on 24 Oct (system demo backup recording, #657).', 'Liu Jin → Yorik'],
    ['Why Testcontainers rather than H2?', 'Our rules live in the database: unique keys, ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, guarded updates and role grants. H2 accepts different SQL, so we test on real PostgreSQL 18, Redis 8.10 and ElasticMQ in containers. Example: OrderRepositoryIT.duplicatePaymentIsRejectedAtTheDatabase. The cost is Docker and time: the build-quality job takes about 13 minutes (B8).', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['Do you do TDD?', 'For some changes. Four day logs record the red step: new PostgreSQL tests for the offer query failed first (27 Sep, #205); four admin-console changes started from failing tests (1 Oct, PRs #326–#329); the rate-limit tests failed first, 429 expected, 200 returned (3 Oct); two failing tests reproduced the join-quote bug before the fix (7 Oct). Commits are squashed, so the red step lives in the day logs (docs/agile/practices.md). Other changes add their tests in the same pull request, and the 80% new-code gate requires them.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['What is your test coverage?', 'SonarCloud on dev, read 24 Oct (analyses of bef39b72, the last code change on dev): backend 99.4% and frontend 96.1% overall, both gates passed; on new code 99.3% and 96.2%. The gate needs at least 80% on new code (NFR-06). The frontend figure excludes the generated API client, the route tree and the app entry files (ci-frontend.yml, sonar.coverage.exclusions). Coverage does not measure assertion quality, and we run no mutation testing.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['How do you test the frontend against the API?', 'Contract first. The backend regenerates contracts/openapi.json, and CI fails if the committed file is out of date; openapi-diff fails a breaking change unless it is labelled. The frontend generates its client and its MSW handlers from that file (frontend ADRs 0003, 0011), and an unhandled request fails a test. The stubs prove only the frontend side.', 'Liu Jin → Lim'],
    ['Why is the performance test not on dev?', 'Dev is one host, and its database runs on the same machine, so a load test would slow down the demo system and its database; the k6 README forbids it. The test runs on the SWE5001 ECS environment, with an image built from dev on 6 October (slide 21; the 16 Oct run is on slide 31). The local Node workload ran on 30 Sep (docs/perf/sprint3-baseline.md). Neither result is from dev, and each is labelled.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['Does CloudFront cache the API, so k6 measures the cache?', 'No. The /api/* behaviour is uncached, and the k6 preflight checks that before a run (perf/k6/README.md). Every request reaches the ALB and the ECS service.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['Is 200 requests a second the same as 100 concurrent users?', 'Not directly, so we convert. k6 sends requests at a set rate; the proposal counts users. By Little\'s law, N = X(R + Z): 100 users who wait 0.5 s and get answers in about 30 ms send about 190 requests a second, below our 200. Our limit is p95 under 500 ms, four times stricter than 2 s. The 0.5 s wait is our assumption. The local run on 30 Sep used exactly 100 users with 200 ms waits, and every p95 was 57 ms or less.', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['Is the last-seat race test a unit test?', 'No, it is an integration test. StripeLocalIntegrationTest.twentyBuyersRaceForLastSeatAndNineteenRefundObligationsPersist sends twenty paid buyers through the production intent and fulfilment ports against PostgreSQL 18 in Testcontainers. StripeCheckoutFlowTest.concurrentPaidBuyersCannotBothTakeLastSeat also uses real PostgreSQL, but its provider, group and order parts are test doubles (class javadoc), so we do not cite it. The unit tests of the same rule are PaidGroupDealTest (the aggregate guards) and DealStatusTest (the transition table).', 'Liu Jin'],
    ['Why no end-user acceptance testing?', 'Quadrant three is manual checks by the team, and they are logged: the Stripe Sandbox browser run (20 Sep, local) and the sign-in cookie check on dev (8 Oct). We had no outside users in the course, so we do not claim UAT.', 'Liu Jin'],
  ] },
  4: { window: 'during the talk / 17:03–17:10', rows: [
    ['Why EC2 and not ECS for dev?', 'Dev reuses one existing EC2 host with the monolith in Docker Compose (operator guide; backend ADR 0008): SWE5006 needs one complete, always-on system at low cost, and the monolith fits one t3.large. Limitation: one host, so a deploy restarts the API (502 on 3–4 Oct), and PostgreSQL and Redis share the host outside the pipeline.', 'Pan'],
    ['How do you roll back?', 'Automatic: deploy.sh health-checks the new container and restarts the previous digest if it fails; the workflow also checks public health. Manual: rollback-backend.yml (reason, confirmation, same concurrency group). Limitation: rollback-backend.yml has never run and no automatic rollback has fired. Flyway is never reversed, so migrations stay backward-compatible.', 'Pan'],
    ['How are secrets handled?', 'CI holds no AWS keys: OIDC gives short-lived roles per job. The repository has three Actions secrets, none for AWS or the app (SONAR_TOKEN, TYPESAFE_API_KEY, BOARD_SNAPSHOT_TOKEN; gh secret list, 19 Oct). Dev application secrets live in /etc/blocbuy/backend.env (root, 0600). Limitation: the account still has two IAM users for SSM port forwarding, one with an active key (governance README).', 'Pan'],
    ['How long from merge to live?', 'Medians from gh on 19 Oct: backend about 3.1 minutes from the push run starting to the deploy job ending (88 runs); frontend about 2.7 minutes including the e2e-dev smoke (94 runs). PR checks take about 16 minutes for backend and 5 for frontend. Per-workflow table: B8.', 'Pan'],
    ['Do you check in to trunk at least daily?', 'No. EPAT 07 asks for that; we use Branch by Feature instead: one short branch per story, and the ruleset makes every branch be up to date with dev and pass the required checks before it merges. 342 pull requests merged into dev from 14 Sep to 22 Oct, but a daily check-in is not shown (docs/agile/practices.md, continuous integration).', 'Pan'],
    ['How do you manage dependencies?', 'Dependabot checks Maven, npm, Docker and GitHub Actions daily with a cooldown and grouped updates (SpotBugs engine and plugin move together since PR #580); majors of Spring, Java, React and TypeScript are ignored on purpose. Every update passes the same required checks. Trivy scans dependencies on change and weekly. Actions are pinned to commit SHAs.', 'Pan → Lim'],
    ['Is the deployed image the one you tested?', 'Not byte for byte. The squash merge and the strict up-to-date rule make the merged tree the tested tree, but package-dev rebuilds it with -DskipTests and publish-ecr-dev builds the delivery image again without repeating the Trivy image gate; ECR scans it on push, a separate control that does not block this job. SonarCloud still analyses dev after the merge, and the deploy is health-checked and smoke-tested, so this is an artifact-assurance limit, not an unchecked deploy. It falls short of "build once, promote"; frontend ADR 0004 still describes promotion (open item 18).', 'Pan'],
    ['Is this Continuous Delivery or Continuous Deployment?', 'Continuous Delivery (EPAT 07 CI p34–35: "Deploy to Production is manually triggered"). Production is our shared SWE5001 release: a promotion PR into prod needs a CODEOWNERS approval and three required checks. Every merge deploys automatically to dev, the demo environment, after the code owner approves.', 'Pan'],
    ['Do you run DAST? And produce an SBOM?', 'DAST: a passive OWASP ZAP baseline scans dev weekly, on demand and whenever the scan itself changes, not on ordinary application PRs (dast-baseline.yml, #607): selected anonymous pages and a form-disabled crawl, no active, authenticated or payment testing. Findings are advisory, not a merge gate, while a scanner or coverage failure fails the job; the first hosted run on 22 Oct found 0 High, 2 Medium and 4 Low, all CSP and header settings tracked in #610. A dispatch on dev on 24 Oct (run 40107924516) completed with the same findings, after Pan pinned a rule add-on whose download had failed earlier that day (#682). SBOM: sbom.yml generates CycloneDX SBOMs and licence inventories of the backend Maven compile and runtime dependencies and the frontend pnpm production closure, per release tag, weekly, on request and when a PR is given the run-sbom label, not on ordinary merges (#609); a missing or invalid inventory fails the job, while licence findings are advisory. A PR run on an earlier revision listed 195 of 198 backend third-party artifacts (three optional ones, not in the jar) and all 96 frontend production package versions (94 distinct packages); a dispatch on dev on 24 Oct (run 40076604904) passed on the merged revision. It is a source inventory, not an inventory of the exact deployed JAR, image or bundle. Both limits are in B9.', 'Pan'],
  ] },
  5: { window: '17:03–17:10', rows: [
    ['Why is there no auto-scaling on dev?', 'Dev is one EC2 host running the monolith in Docker Compose, with PostgreSQL and Redis on the host (backend ADR 0008), so SWE5006 needs one complete, always-on system at low cost. Auto-scaling exists only in the SWE5001 topology on ECS (commerce 2–6 tasks on ALB requests per target, read 21 Oct), which we do not claim as tested scale (the V3 run was rejected); the proposal sets no scalability target. Backup B12 shows the SWE5001 scale-out run (commerce 1 → 3 tasks, 19 Oct). Limitation: the dev host is a single point of failure.', 'Yorik → Pan'],
    ['Is the refund real?', 'A real Stripe Sandbox refund: the Refund API in test mode, so no money moves. Locally on 20 Sep, refund re_3UHbeV… reached succeeded and a recheck created no duplicate (B11). On dev on 23 Oct, a natural expiry closed a two-seat bloc 18.9 s after its deadline and one S$22 Sandbox refund succeeded 40.6 s after it, once (PR #664, backend/docs/payment/stripe-dev-recovery-acceptance-20261023.md).', 'Yorik'],
    ['What happens if Stripe is down?', 'The Checkout attempt is saved before Stripe is called, so a timeout leaves one recoverable attempt with a stable provider key, never a second payment (StripeCheckoutFlowTest). Refunds stay as durable obligations; the worker retries every 30 s with the same idempotency key, and after 23 hours it only searches Stripe and hands mismatches to a person.', 'Yorik'],
    ['How do you avoid a double payment?', 'A repeated Checkout request with the same buyer, intent and Idempotency-Key reuses the payment. Webhook events are stored by event ID, so a replay changes nothing (StripeLocalIntegrationTest.eightConcurrentWebhookReplaysStillProduceOneMemberAndOneOrder). When paid buyers race for the last seat, one gets it and every other gets a refund obligation (StripeLocalIntegrationTest.twentyBuyersRaceForLastSeatAndNineteenRefundObligationsPersist, local).', 'Yorik → Eric'],
    ['Why did the demo not show notifications?', 'By decision: the dev host runs no message broker, so the outbox writer, relay and consumer are off (application-ec2.yml; ADR 0007 scope decision, 11 Oct). Notifications run on the SWE5001 environment through SNS and SQS, and locally and in CI against ElasticMQ; backup B19 shows the relay’s Template Method.', 'Pan'],
    ['Why not race two buyers for the last seat on dev?', 'Because a live race is not repeatable on shared dev and would leave a refund obligation on a real account. The race is a test that runs on every pull request that changes the backend: twenty paid buyers race for the last seat of a team of two through the production intent and fulfilment ports against PostgreSQL 18 in Testcontainers; the team ends with 2 members, 2 orders, current_size 2 and 19 refund obligations (StripeLocalIntegrationTest.twentyBuyersRaceForLastSeatAndNineteenRefundObligationsPersist). It is a local test, and we say so (step D6).', 'Yorik → Eric'],
    ['How do you know expiry happens within one minute on dev?', 'By design, by a local load measurement and by one dev measurement. TeamExpirySweepJob runs every minute (fixedDelay PT1M) under a database lease and expires every open team whose deadline has passed; the local run of 30 Sep measured a worst lag of 58.18 s for 30 teams under 100-user load. On dev on 23 Oct one bloc expired 18.9 s after its deadline and was refunded 40.6 s after it, read from the database (backend/docs/payment/stripe-dev-recovery-acceptance-20261023.md); that is one run, not a bound. On screen the Refunded row shows the closing day, not the time (“Closed 26 Oct”), and the receipt shows the refund status, so the demo shows the outcome: the 16:05 bloc, due ≈ 16:45, is refunded by the time we look.', 'Yorik'],
    ['Is 401 vs 403 vs 400 deliberate?', 'Yes. The Sa-Token interceptor checks login on every /api/v1 path except anonymous GETs of listings and invites, so no session is 401 COMMON_401_001; a signed-in buyer on an admin endpoint fails StpUtil.checkRole(ADMIN) and gets 403 COMMON_403_001; a money write without the X-BlocBuy-Request marker is 400 COMMON_400_001, and a wrong value is 403 (StripeCheckoutController.checkCsrf). Mapping: GlobalExceptionHandler.', 'Yorik → Liu Jin'],
    ['(Questions parked during the talk)', 'Lim reads each parked question from the backchannel in one line and names the owner; those come first. Answer each in 30–45 s: decision, reason, evidence or limitation.', 'Lim'],
  ] },
};

